# Why do costs rise as you produce more — or fall?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What are fixed, variable, average and marginal costs? Why does marginal cost cut average cost at its lowest point?"
next: {"title":"What is fixed in the short run?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/110-what-is-fixed-in-the-short-run.cast"}

## Cheaper or dearer?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - 1st builder
      - 2nd
      - 3rd
      - 4th
    values:
      - 4
      - 3
      - 2
      - 1
    value_labels: true
    bar_colors: same
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 5
    y_label: Extra boats a month
    x_label: One shed, one more builder
    gap: 0.4
    label_size: 24
sources:
    - id: modelt
      title: Ford Model T (prices and production by year)
      authors: Wikipedia, from Ford production records
      year: 2025
      finding: '1909: Runabout from $825, 10,666 built; 1923: 2,011,125 built; 1925: price lowered to $260 (dollars of the day).'
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ford_Model_T

    icon car size 130 set twemoji x 260 y 430 icon_look picture of car hidden true
    text t1909 "1909: $825 · 10,666 built" cites ["modelt"] x 610 y 500 font_size 38 hidden true
    text t1923 "1923: 2 million a year" cites ["modelt"] x 610 y 420 font_size 38 hidden true
    text t1925 "1925: $260" #b5482e cites ["modelt"] x 610 y 340 font_size 38 hidden true
    text rush "Small yard in a rush: each extra boat dearer" #2f6b8f x 500 y 230 font_size 36 hidden true
    text dimret "diminishing returns" #b5482e x 720 y 560 font_size 36 hidden true
    scratch calc at.percent [70,66] work ["$4,000 ÷ 4 boats = $1,000 a boat","$4,000 ÷ 1 boat = $4,000 a boat"] font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Cheaper or dearer?"

Does making more of something make each one cheaper, o (@draw car@)r dearer? Start with Ford's Model T.

B: In 1909 Ford built about ten tho (@draw t1909@)usand of them, and the cheapest cost eight hundred and twenty-five dollars.

By 1923 Ford wa (@draw t1923@)s building two million a year, and by 1925 the car cost two hundred and sixty dol (@draw t1925 ends@)lars.

B: So more means cheaper. Except ask a small yar (@draw rush@)d racing to finish orders before summer: every extra boat costs more than the last.

    erase car t1909 t1923 t1925 rush

Both are right. To see why, leave the big boatyard and take Kai's dinghy yard across the harbour. Illustrative numbers: one shed, one slipway, and as many builders as Kai cares to hire.
    draw axes

One builder al (@draw bar_1@)one turns out four dinghies a month. A (@draw bar_2@) second adds three more. A third adds (@draw bar_3 ends@) two.

    ask "Drag the last bar to the extra boats a fourth builder adds." on bar_4 store g default "2" voice b
        intro: Your turn. How many extra boats does a fourth builder add?
        right: One: close to your {g:0}.
        wrong: You said {g:0}: just one.

Just one. The shed doesn't grow, so each newcomer waits for the slipway and shares the same tools.
    highlight bar_4 effect box

That's dimin (@draw dimret@)ishing returns again: the shed is fixed, so each builder adds less.

    erase dimret

B: Now put a price on it. Every builder earns the same wage, say four thousand dollars a month. (@draw calc_box calc_line_1@) The first builder's four boat (@highlight bar_1 effect box@)s cost a thousand dollars each.

The fourth bu (@draw calc_line_2@)ilder's single boat costs  (@highlight bar_4 effect box@)the whole four thousand. Each extra boat costs more, because each extra builder adds less.

    pause 0.4

## Average and marginal
use: firm_cost_curves
with:
    mode: competition
    show:
      - mc
      - atc
      - avc
    fixed_cost: 400
    mark_minima: true
    x_label: Boats a month (Q)
    y_label: ' '

    text ylab "Cost\nper boat" #3d3833 x 45 y 690 font_size 24 hidden true
    text fixedcue "rent: fixed" #8f887c at.data [72,88] font_size 32 hidden true
    text varcue "wages: variable" #8f887c at.data [72,80] font_size 32 hidden true
    dot g1 at.data [25,49.5] hidden true
    dot g3 at.data [75,48.8] hidden true
    text fallnote "MC < ATC: average falls" #2f6b8f at.data [24,7] font_size 36 hidden true
    text risenote "MC > ATC: average rises" #b5482e at.data [76,7] font_size 36 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"rest"}] then rest hidden true
    scratch mathbox at.percent [50,78] work ["MC = w ÷ MP","ATC = F ÷ Q + AVC","slope of ATC = (MC − ATC) ÷ Q"] font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Average and marginal"

    hide label_min_atc label_min_avc q_star p_star label_q_star label_p_star

A rising cost of the next boat is only half the bill. The other half is the shed.
    draw axes ylab

B: Its rent, say eight thousand dollars a month, is due whether the yard builds one boat or twent (@draw fixedcue ends@)y. That's a fixed cost.

The wages gr (@draw varcue@)ow with every boat: a variable cost. Share them out over the b (@draw avc label_avc@)oats, and you get the average variable cost, AVC.

Now add the rent, shared over th (@draw atc label_atc@)e boats too, and you get the average total cost of a boat, ATC. At first it falls fast, because the rent is split ever thinner.

    erase fixedcue varcue

B: Then it turns up again. (@draw g1 min_atc g3 parallel true@) Somewhere the rising cost of the next boat takes over.

    ask "Where does the marginal-cost curve cross ATC? Tap one of the three dots." choose ["g1","min_atc","g3"] answer min_atc voice b
        intro: Where do you think the cost of one more boat, the marginal cost, crosses ATC?
        right: The bottom of ATC. While the next boat costs less than the average it drags the average down; past the bottom it pulls it up.
        wrong: Ask what happens to an average when the next number is below it, and when it is above: here, the bottom of ATC.

    erase g1 g3

Right (@draw mc label_mc@) at the bottom. And it has to, for the same reason as the average mark in a stack of exams.

B: A test be (@draw fallnote@)low your average pulls the average down. A  (@draw risenote@)test above it pulls the average up.

Same with boats. While the next boat costs less than the average, the average falls. Once it costs more, the average ris (@highlight min_atc effect pulse@)es. So MC cuts ATC, and AVC, at the (@draw min_avc@)ir lowest points.

    erase fallnote risenote

Try it: raise the shed's (@animate fixed_cost 900 duration 3 ghost ["atc"]@) rent, and watch ATC climb while MC doesn't move at all.

    explore params ["fixed_cost"]

    erase atc_ghost

Three lines of math sit behind these curves. Open them, or skip them.
    draw mathq

    ask "The math behind the cost curves: show it, or skip it?" on mathq store m default "Skip it" stage own

@math

    fade axes mc atc avc label_mc label_atc label_avc min_atc min_avc ylab to 0.25

    erase mathq

One more boat needs one over MP extra builders, so it costs the wage divided by the marginal product.
    draw mathbox_box mathbox_line_1

Average  (@draw mathbox_line_2@)total cost is the rent spread over Q, plus the average of the wages.

And averag (@draw mathbox_line_3@)e cost slopes down while MC is below it, up while above, and is flat exactly where they meet.

    pause 0.5

    erase mathbox

@rest

    fade axes mc atc avc label_mc label_atc label_avc min_atc min_avc ylab to 1

    erase mathq

B: So the rent decides whether the yard makes money. It has nothing to say about what one more boat costs.
    highlight mc effect pulse

    pause 0.4

## Bigger sheds
domain: {"x":[0,100],"y":[12,62]}

    axes axes x_label "Boats a month (Q)" y_label "Average cost per boat" cross corner ticks.x [] ticks.y [] hidden true
    curve s1 #8f887c expr "0.01*(x-50)^2 + 30 + 0.03*(x-20)^2" x_from 5 x_to 44 hidden true
    text s1_l "small shed" #8f887c at.data [16,60] font_size 30 hidden true
    curve s2 #8f887c expr "0.01*(x-50)^2 + 30 + 0.03*(x-50)^2" x_from 25 x_to 75 hidden true
    text s2_l "medium shed" #8f887c at.data [50,58] font_size 30 hidden true
    curve s3 #8f887c expr "0.01*(x-50)^2 + 30 + 0.03*(x-80)^2" x_from 56 x_to 95 hidden true
    text s3_l "big shed" #8f887c at.data [86,60] font_size 30 hidden true
    curve lrac #b5482e expr "0.01*(x-50)^2 + 30" x_from 5 x_to 95 style.stroke_width 5 hidden true
    text lrac_l "LRAC" #b5482e at.data [97,45] font_size 34 hidden true
    text eos "economies of scale" #2f6b8f at.data [22,22] font_size 32 hidden true
    text dis "diseconomies of scale" #b5482e at.data [78,22] font_size 32 hidden true
    scratch geo at.percent [74,22] work ["Tank, every side ×2:","steel ×4, holds ×8"] font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Bigger sheds"

Rent that never moves is a feature of the short run. Given a few years, the yard can build a bigger shed.
    draw axes s1 s1_l

B: And every shed size gets i (@draw s2 s2_l@)ts own U. A bigger shed costs more rent, but it builds a lot of boats che (@draw s3 s3_l ends@)aply.

For each number of boats, Kai picks, out of every shed size he could build, the one with th (@draw lrac lrac_l@)e lowest curve there. Trace that lowest edge, and you get the long-run average cost, LRAC.

B: It wraps around the U's from below, like an envelope.
    highlight lrac effect pulse

Where it falls, there are econom (@draw eos@)ies of scale: double the shed, the tools and the crew, and you get more than double the boats.

Partly that's geometry. Double ever (@draw geo_box geo_line_1@)y side of a tank, and it takes four times (@draw geo_line_2@) the steel but holds eight times as much.

    erase geo

B: And partly it's people. In a big yard one builder can do nothing but hulls, another nothing but rigging, and each gets very good at it.
    highlight eos effect underline

    hide eos axes s1 s1_l s2 s2_l s3 s3_l lrac lrac_l

    cards sortc check each
        Cost falls with size: Bigger tanks, Specialised workers, Bulk timber
        Cost rises with size: Layers of managers, Slow decisions, Harder to watch workers

    ask "Cheaper or dearer as the yard grows? Sort each card." intro "Your turn: sort these." on sortc store f stage own voice b
        right: All six: sharing, specialising and bulk lower the cost; management strain raises it.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try. Here, tanks, specialists and bulk buying cut costs; managers, slow decisions and monitoring add them.

    erase sortc

    show axes s1 s1_l s2 s2_l s3 s3_l lrac lrac_l

Where the envelope turns up (@draw eos dis@), diseconomies of scale: more layers of managers, slower decisions, and nobody quite sure who is doing what.

So average cost falls when the yard shares out what's fixed or grows everything together, and rises when something can't keep up.
    highlight lrac effect pulse

    pause 0.4

## Getting better at it
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Henry Ford would hire you.
    good: Most of the curve, and all of the shape.
    poor: Even the Model T took a few tries.
    none: No answers? Then your average stays where it was.
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - '1'
      - '2'
      - '4'
      - '8'
      - '16'
    series:
      - {name: planes, values: [100, 80, 64, 51, 41]}
    points: true
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 110
    x_label: Planes built so far
    y_label: Hours per plane (1st = 100; ideal 80% curve)
    box:
      x: 70
      'y': 120
      w: 590
      h: 540
    y_ticks:
      - 0
      - 20
      - 40
      - 60
      - 80
      - 100
sources:
    - id: wright
      title: Factors Affecting the Cost of Airplanes
      authors: Wright, T. P.
      year: 1936
      finding: In aircraft production, each doubling of the cumulative number of planes built cut the labour time per plane by about 20% (an '80% curve').
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Experience_curve_effects
    - id: owid
      title: Why did renewables become so cheap so fast?
      authors: Roser, M. (Our World in Data)
      year: 2020
      finding: 'The learning rate of solar PV modules is 20.2%: each doubling of cumulative installed capacity cut the module price by about a fifth.'
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/cheap-renewables-growth
    - id: nemet
      title: 'Beyond the learning curve: factors influencing cost reductions in photovoltaics'
      authors: Nemet, G. F.
      year: 2006
      finding: 'A bottom-up cost model of PV, 1975–2001: plant size, module efficiency and the cost of silicon drove most of the cost fall; learning from experience only weakly explains changes in them. Energy Policy 34(17): 3218–3232.'
    - id: johnston
      title: Statistical Cost Analysis
      authors: Johnston, J.
      year: 1960
      finding: 'Long-run cost curves estimated for a range of industries were closer to L-shaped than U-shaped: average cost falls and then flattens rather than rising again.'

    text drop "−20% per doubling" #b5482e cites ["wright"] x 790 y 580 font_size 38 hidden true
    text lc "learning curve" x 790 y 510 font_size 36 hidden true
    text solar "Solar: −20% per doubling" #2f6b8f cites ["owid"] x 790 y 420 font_size 30 hidden true
    link solarlink href https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/casts/solar-panel-prices.cast title "Solar drawcast" form text x 840 y 360 hidden true
    text nem "Nemet: scale, efficiency, silicon" cites ["nemet"] x 820 y 250 font_size 28 hidden true
    text sure1 "Scale cuts cost:" #3d3833 x 810 y 400 font_size 34 hidden true
    text sure2 "U or L? contested" #b5482e cites ["johnston"] x 810 y 290 font_size 34 hidden true
    text sure3 "Learning? contested" #b5482e cites ["nemet"] x 810 y 200 font_size 34 hidden true
    text sure1b "mostly agreed" #2f6b8f x 810 y 350 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Getting better at it"

B: But the Model T got cheaper partly for another reason, one that none of these curves can show.
    draw axes

In 1936 an aircraft engineer, Theodore Wright, tracked how many hours of work each new plane took.
    highlight axes

Every time the t (@draw line_1@)otal number of planes ever built doubled, the labour for each new one fell by about a fifth. Here is that rule as an ideal curve, illus (@draw drop ends@)trative.

B: So cost depends not just on how much you make this month, but on how much you have ever made. That's (@draw lc@) a learning curve.

Solar panels hav (@draw solar@)e followed the same rule: about twenty percent cheaper for each doubling of all the panels ever installed. A whole dr (@draw solarlink@)awcast tells that story.

B: Is that really learning, though? Or just bigger factories and cheaper silicon?
    highlight solar effect underline

Gregory Nemet took solar costs apa (@draw nem@)rt in 2006. Bigger plants, more efficient cells and cheaper silicon did most of the work, and learning by doing explains them only weakly.

    erase solar solarlink nem lc

So how sure are we? That bigger operations make each unit ch (@draw sure1 sure1b@)eaper in many industries: mostly agreed.

B: That average cost turns up again for gian (@draw sure2@)ts: contested. Since the 1950s many studies find it flattens out instead, more an L than a U. And how much of a learning curve is re (@draw sure3@)ally learning is contested too.

    quiz "A half-empty ferry takes on one more foot passenger. What does that passenger add to the ferry's costs?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: Almost nothing: the crossing, the fuel and the crew are paid for anyway, so the marginal cost is near zero.
        wrong: Ask what changes when one more person walks on: here, almost nothing.
        + Almost nothing
        * The average cost per passenger
        * A full share of fuel and crew

    quiz "A factory's next unit costs $8, and its average cost so far is $10. Make one more, and its average cost…" voice b
        right: Falls. A unit cheaper than the average pulls the average down, like a test score below your average.
        wrong: Think of a test score below your average: the average falls.
        + falls
        * rises
        * stays at $10

    quiz "A shipyard's 100th ship took 1,000 hours of work. With a 20% learning curve, about how long will its 200th take?"
        right: 800 hours. From the 100th to the 200th is one doubling, so a fifth comes off.
        wrong: One doubling, one fifth off: 800 hours.
        * 1,000 hours
        + 800 hours
        * 500 hours

So why do costs rise, or fall? They rise when you crowd more work onto something that can't grow. They fall when you share out what's fixed, grow everything together, or simply get better with practice.
    highlight line_1 effect pulse

    pause 0.5

